From 167f3e0779e0ee284a73d77518830cec9991d77a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 05:26:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] driver: encode H.264 and HEVC on V4L2 stateful encoders

Offer VAEntrypointEncSlice for H.264 (Constrained Baseline, Main,
High) and HEVC Main when a V4L2 memory-to-memory encoder is present: a
device that takes NV12 on OUTPUT and produces H.264 or HEVC on CAPTURE,
such as the Apple video encoder (apple-ave) of M-series Macs. Screen
recorders and browsers that encode through VA-API then use the hardware
encoder with no configuration.

A stateful encoder picks the picture structure and writes the parameter
sets itself, so the driver offers what such an encoder does on its own:
one forward reference and no backward one (clients then use I and P
pictures only), the client's intra period as the encoder's GOP with a
forced IDR for any other IDR the client asks for, and no packed headers
(the encoder writes its SPS/PPS/VPS in band before every IDR). CQP maps
to the encoder's constant-quality mode with the requested QP, VBR and
CBR to its bit-rate modes.

As with video processing, every picture is one synchronous job:
vaEndPicture() queues the input surface's dma-buf, waits for the coded
picture and copies it into the coded buffer. A surface whose backing is
larger than the surface (rounded-up allocations) is coded as the
surface size through the encoder's crop selection when it has one.

V4L2R_NO_ENCODE=1 hides the encoder.
